auto-retry failed test cases:
in playwright.config.js file, comment out 'retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,' add 'retries: 2,' it means if a test case fails, it will auto-run max twice more
until it succeeds. 
OR:
add '--retries=3' in command line when run tests.
if a test case fails at the first time and succeeds in any retry, it will be marked as 'flaky'.
it will show 'Retry #1' and 'Retry #2' following 'Run' tab in test report.

official docs:
https://playwright.dev/docs/ci-intro


to always open test report after execution, add this part into playwright.config.ts file:
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  reporter: [
    ['html', { open: 'always' }] // Force the HTML report to always open
  ],
});


hard assertion and soft assertion:
when executing multiple tests in one file, once one step fails, it doesnt execute following steps, which is called hard assertion as a default setting in Playwright.
but sometimes we fail some test cases on purpose. to keep executing the following steps, apply soft assertion by:
expect(locator).toBeVisible()  ->  expect.soft(locator).toBeVisible()


save test reports in our local machine:
in playwright.config.ts file:
export default defineConfig({
  reporter: [
    ['html', { outputFolder: 'test-reports/report-${timestamp}', open: 'always' }]     //to avoid overriding the previous test report 
  ],
});

the difference of outputFolder and outputDir is: 
outputFolder stores test results
outputDir stores test artifacts like screenshots, videos and trace etc.

if test results are not stored as specified in outputFolder, that would be it's reading from some environment variables set earlier in powershell:
$env:PLAYWRIGHT_HTML_OUTPUT_DIR="playwright-report/run-$(Get-Date -UFormat 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')"; npx playwright test
sth like that.. to remove it:
$ Remove-Item Env: \PLAYWRIGHT_HTML_OUTPUT_DIR



